        "---\n",
        "## Section 6 — Word Vectors and Distributional Semantics\n",
        "\n",
        "### The Distributional Hypothesis\n",
        "\n",
        "> *\"You shall know a word by the company it keeps\"* — J.R. Firth, 1957\n",
        "\n",
        "Words that appear in similar contexts have similar meanings.  \n",
        "We can **represent** meaning as a vector of numbers — words with similar meanings will point in similar directions in vector space.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Cosine Similarity\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\cos\\text{-sim}(A, B) = \\frac{A \\cdot B}{|A| \\times |B|}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "- $= 1$: identical direction (same meaning)\n",
        "- $= 0$: orthogonal (unrelated)\n",
        "- $= -1$: opposite direction (opposite meaning)\n",
        "\n",
        "Why cosine and not Euclidean distance? Cosine ignores vector **magnitude** — a rare word (`\"kitten\"`) and a common word (`\"cat\"`) can still be semantically close even if their raw count vectors differ in magnitude.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "2ac7157b"
      }
    },
