        "### Challenge 2 — Trigram Language Model (10 points)\n",
        "\n",
        "Extend the bigram model from Section 4 to a **trigram model**.\n",
        "\n",
        "$$P(w_3 \\mid w_1, w_2) = \\frac{\\text{count}(w_1, w_2, w_3)}{\\text{count}(w_1, w_2)}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Compare trigram perplexity vs bigram perplexity on the same 5 held-out sentences.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ac59cfe9"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "68913a27",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# ── Challenge 2: Trigram Language Model ──────────────────────────────────────\n",
        "\n",
        "# TODO C2a: Build trigram_counts — Counter of all (w1, w2, w3) triples in corpus_tokens\n",
        "trigram_counts = Counter(zip(corpus_tokens, corpus_tokens[1:], corpus_tokens[2:]))\n",
        "\n",
        "# TODO C2b: Implement smoothed_trigram_prob(w3, w2, w1) with Laplace smoothing\n",
        "#            P(w3|w1,w2) = (count(w1,w2,w3)+1) / (count(w1,w2)+V)\n",
        "def smoothed_trigram_prob(w3, w2, w1):\n",
        "    count_w1_w2_w3 = trigram_counts[(w1, w2, w3)]\n",
        "    count_w1_w2 = bigram_counts[(w1, w2)]\n",
        "    # If the bigram (w1, w2) was never seen, its count is 0. Denominator becomes V.\n",
        "    # If the bigram was seen, denominator is its count + V.\n",
        "    denominator = count_w1_w2 + V\n",
        "    return (count_w1_w2_w3 + 1) / denominator\n",
        "\n",
        "# TODO C2c: Implement trigram_perplexity(sentence)\n",
        "#            Uses log-sum of smoothed_trigram_prob over all consecutive triples\n",
        "def trigram_perplexity(sentence):\n",
        "    tokens = sentence.split()\n",
        "    if len(tokens) < 3:\n",
        "        # Perplexity is undefined or very high for sentences shorter than n-gram length\n",
        "        return float('inf')\n",
        "\n",
        "    log_prob = 0.0\n",
        "    # Iterate over consecutive token triples\n",
        "    for w1, w2, w3 in zip(tokens, tokens[1:], tokens[2:]):\n",
        "        prob = smoothed_trigram_prob(w3, w2, w1)\n",
        "        if prob == 0: # Avoid log(0), although Laplace smoothing should prevent this\n",
        "            return float('inf')\n",
        "        log_prob += math.log(prob)\n",
        "\n",
        "    N = len(tokens) # N is the total number of tokens in the sequence\n",
        "    return math.exp(-log_prob / N)\n",
        "\n",
        "# ── Compare bigram vs trigram perplexity ─────────────────────────────────────\n",
        "print(f\"{'Sentence':<55} | {'Bigram PP':>10} | {'Trigram PP':>10}\")\n",
        "print(\"-\" * 80)\n",
        "for s in test_sentences:\n",
        "    bi_pp  = sentence_perplexity(s, bigram_counts, unigram_counts, V)\n",
        "    tri_pp = trigram_perplexity(s)\n",
        "    print(f\"{s[:55]:<55} | {bi_pp:10.2f} | {tri_pp:10.2f}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# TODO C2d: Write 2-3 sentences explaining which model scores lower perplexity\n",
        "#            and WHY (hint: more context = better predictions, but also more sparsity)\n",
        "print(\"\\nAnalysis:\")\n",
        "print(\"The trigram model generally scores lower perplexity, indicating it is less 'surprised' by the test sentences. This is because it uses more context (the two preceding words) to predict the next word, allowing it to capture more nuanced linguistic patterns and dependencies than the bigram model. However, trigram models are also more susceptible to data sparsity, meaning many possible trigrams might not appear in the training corpus, which Laplace smoothing helps to mitigate by ensuring no probability is zero.\")"
      ],
